    "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. Confirmation uses worked-solution evidence; no submitted-answer grades were supplied. q-80460: integrating each component gives t^3 and e^t, plus an arbitrary vector constant. q-99350: the second component integrates to -2 cos(2t) by the chain rule. q-50078: the component antiderivatives are -e^(-t) and sec(t). q-99366: evaluating sqrt(2) sin(t) and tan(t) between 0 and pi/4 gives (1,1). q-99517: integration gives ((t-1)^3+c1,t^4-t+c2), and the initial condition sets both constants to 1. q-5570: five distinct marbles in an ordered row give 40·39·38·37·36=78,960,960 permutations. q-85048: P(45,0)=45!/45!=1. q-7282: x(t)=9t+C and x(1)=0 give C=-9. q-102993: integrating acceleration and applying v(1)=-1/2 gives v(t)=-2/(t+1)^2; integrating again and applying x(1)=1 gives x(t)=2/(t+1). q-29438: cotangent is zero when its argument is pi/2+n*pi, so x=5*pi/6+n*pi. q-117692: cotangent has poles when 3x=180n degrees, giving x=60n degrees. q-117942: Euler's method with step size 1 produces (0,2), (1,2), (2,4). This confirms the requested numerical approximation even though the exact solution does not extend to x=2. q-120940: Euler's method with step size 1 produces y-values 2,2,4,12 at x=1,2,3,4. q-47787: only [1,5] is a closed interval on which log_2(x) is defined and continuous. q-73499: only [-1,0] avoids the discontinuity at x=1 and has a continuous function on the entire closed interval. The source's term 'essential discontinuity' describes its nonremovable discontinuity; the right-hand limit is negative infinity. q-49273: on the original integrand's domain, simplification gives cos(x)+csc^2(x), whose antiderivative is sin(x)-cot(x)+C.",
